--TEST--
A `Core\Test` assertion loop whose own ledger grows past `[limits] memory` stops with the limit report, not an empty `FATAL: ` — `rule:errors/on-limit`, `rule:errors/an-allocation-past-the-ceiling-is-refused-in-front-of-itself`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every assertion adds an entry to the ledger, so the ledger's own storage is
// what crosses the ceiling. The request still holds those bytes when the run
// reports the `FATAL`, and the report's text is built past the ceiling.
int $i = 0;
while ($i < 2000000) {
    Core\Test::assertSame($i, $i);
    $i = $i + 1;
}
echo "finished\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The ceiling the ledger above crosses, and nothing else.
[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECT--
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the program exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 16777216, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler
help: raise `memory` under `[limits]` in nvs.toml, or call `Core\Config::set('memory', '32M')` in the program
